<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">West Nusa Tenggara</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">West Nusa Tenggara is a province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> made up of the islands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombok">Lombok</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumbawa">Sumbawa</a>, with its capital at Mataram on Lombok. Formed in 1958, it includes the lands of the former sultanates of Sumbawa, Dompu and Bima. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-tambora">Mount Tambora</a>, whose eruption in 1815 was the largest in recorded history, stands on Sumbawa, and Mount Rinjani on Lombok.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/west-nusa-tenggara.jpg" alt="West Nusa Tenggara" width="320" height="147" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:West_Nusa_Tenggara_in_Indonesia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>West Nusa Tenggara comprises the western portion of the Lesser Sunda Islands, with the exception of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a>, which is its own province. Its area of 19,675.89 km2 consists of two main islands, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombok">Lombok</a> in the west and the much larger but less densely populated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumbawa">Sumbawa</a> in the east, with several small islands such as the Gili Islands. Mount Rinjani, an active volcano on Lombok, is the highest mountain in the province at 3,726 metres and has a crater lake called Segara Anak. The climate is tropical, with a rainy season from November to March, and the east of Sumbawa is dry and often short of rain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Prehistoric objects such as sarcophagi, decorated stoneware and axes suggest the region was first inhabited by people from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasak-people">Sasak</a> are the most numerous indigenous people, mostly on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombok">Lombok</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumbawa">Sumbawa</a> has the Sumbawa (Samawa) and Bima. In 1365 the Negarakertagama of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpu-prapanca">Mpu Prapanca</a> listed places on both islands among the lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit Empire</a>. As Majapahit collapsed, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> influence declined and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> spread along the coasts, brought generally by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malays">Malays</a>; in Bima it came with King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdul-kahir">Abdul Kahir</a>, the first Bima king to embrace Islam.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a>'s influence grew in the 17th century. In 1669 Sultan Ambela Abdul Khair Sirajuddin of Bima made an agreement recognising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> power, which barred foreign traders except by Company permit. The eruption of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-tambora">Mount Tambora</a> in 1815 destroyed the two kingdoms of Tambora and Papekat and killed more than 10,000 people. After independence the area belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-east-indonesia">State of East Indonesia</a> and then to the province of the Lesser Sunda Islands. Law No. 64 of 14 August 1958 created West Nusa Tenggara, with Ruslan Djakraningrat as first governor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Mataram, on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombok">Lombok</a>, is the capital and largest city of the province. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">Rice</a>, corn, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soybean">soybeans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peanut">peanuts</a> and tobacco are the main crops, and fishing and seaweed cultivation are important on the coasts. The Batu Hijau mine on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumbawa">Sumbawa</a> is among the world's larger <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and gold mines. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasak-people">Sasak</a> villages of Lombok, Mount Rinjani and the Gili Islands draw many visitors.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “West Nusa Tenggara” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Indonesian independence the sultanates of Sumbawa were dissolved, Dompu becoming a regency of <b>West Nusa Tenggara</b> in 1958.</blockquote><a href="/state/dompu-sultanate">Dompu Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
